{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5","id":"msg_011CdsY6UqC2vcg8RCaqLqWe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Kg9sSm6191qUNo7Q3YY3bY","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-creator-0/113f7cc5-237e-5d6a-8867-a97fd89c58dd/scratchpad/e6/e6-t01/primary/exec-ws/ea-behavior-statement.md","content":"# E-A behavior statement — format.md vs reference.py over probes.json\n\nTask e6-t01, evidence requirement E-A. Author: X-E6 (session with governed\naccess to format.md, reference.py, probes.json — E-A carries no authorship\nrestriction). Probe indices are 0-based over the array in\n`package/artifacts/probes.json`.\n\n## Method\n\nEvery behavior actually exercised by the 10 probes was enumerated, and for\neach one format.md's prose was confronted with reference.py's code. The\nprobe set exercises exactly the behaviors B1–B7 below; no probe exercises\nany behavior outside this list (each probe is assigned to its behaviors\nexplicitly, so coverage is total, not sampled).\n\n## Behaviors exercised by the probe set\n\n### B1 — single-pair rendering `key=value` (probes 0, 2, 3, 4, 8, 9)\nformat.md: \"Each pair is written as key=value.\"\nreference.py: `f\"{esc(k)}={esc(v)}\"`.\n**Agreement.** Both render a pair as escaped-key, `=`, escaped-value.\n\n### B2 — ordering of multiple pairs (probes 1, 5, 6)\nformat.md: \"Pairs appear in the encoded line in the order in which they\nappear in the mapping.\" — i.e. mapping (insertion) order.\nreference.py: `\";\".join(... for k in sorted(d) ...)` — **sorted key\norder**, not mapping order.\n**Determined differently.** Concretely:\n- probe 1 `{\"z\":\"9\",\"a\":\"1\"}`: format.md entails `z=9;a=1`; reference.py\n  returns `a=1;z=9`.\n- probe 5 `{\"m\":\"\",\"\":\"v\"}`: format.md entails `m=;=v`; reference.py\n  returns `=v;m=` (`\"\"` sorts before `\"m\"`).\n- probe 6 `{\"b\":\"2\",\"aa\":\"1\",\"c\":\"3\"}`: format.md entails `b=2;aa=1;c=3`;\n  reference.py returns `aa=1;b=2;c=3`.\nThe two prescriptions coincide only when insertion order happens to equal\nsorted order (probes 0, 2, 3, 4, 7, 8, 9 and any single-pair mapping).\n\n### B3 — escaping of `=` inside keys and values (probes 2, 8)\nformat.md: \"every '=' \\[is written\\] as backslash-equals.\"\nreference.py: `.replace(\"=\", \"\\\\=\")`.\n**Agreement.** probe 2 `{\"note\":\"x=y\"}` → `note=x\\=y`; probe 8\n`{\"eq=key\":\"v1\"}` → `eq\\=key=v1` under both.\n\n### B4 — escaping of `;` inside values (probes 3, 9)\nformat.md: \"every ';' is written as the two characters\nbackslash-semicolon.\"\nreference.py: `.replace(\";\", \"\\\\;\")`.\n**Agreement.** probe 3 `{\"path\":\"a;b\"}` → `path=a\\;b`; probe 9\n`{\"tail\":\"end;\"}` → `tail=end\\;` under both (a trailing `;` in the value\nis escaped like any other).\n\n### B5 — treatment of a literal backslash in a value (probe 4)\nformat.md: the only escaping clause is \"Inside keys and values, every ';'\nis written as the two characters backslash-semicolon and every '=' as\nbackslash-equals.\" No clause addresses the backslash character itself;\nunder the spec as written, characters other than `;` and `=` pass through\nverbatim.\nreference.py: `.replace(\"\\\\\", \"\\\\\\\\\")` applied FIRST — every literal\nbackslash is doubled.\n**Determined differently (and at best underdetermined).** probe 4\n`{\"k\":\"back\\slash\"}` (value contains one literal backslash): a faithful\nreading of format.md yields `k=back\\slash` (backslash unchanged), while\nreference.py returns `k=back\\\\slash` (backslash doubled). Even a reader\nwho suspects backslash ought to be escaped for decodability finds no\nformat.md clause saying so — format.md either determines this behavior\ndifferently from reference.py (verbatim pass-through) or, on the most\ncharitable reading, leaves it undetermined. Either way it fails to\nreproduce reference.py's behavior on probe 4.\n\n### B6 — empty value and empty key (probe 5)\nformat.md: \"Each pair is written as key=value\" with no exclusion of empty\nstrings; an empty value yields `key=`, an empty key yields `=value`.\nreference.py: same — `esc(\"\")` is `\"\"`, so `m=` and `=v` are produced.\n**Agreement on the pair rendering itself** (the pair ORDER on probe 5\ndiffers; that is B2, not B6).\n\n### B7 — empty mapping (probe 7)\nformat.md: \"An empty mapping encodes as the empty line.\" With the function\ncontract \"returns the encoded line as a string\", this is the empty string.\nreference.py: `\";\".join(...)` over zero items → `\"\"`.\n**Agreement.**\n\n## Conclusion (E-A)\n\nOn the probe set, format.md determines the output differently from\nreference.py in exactly two behaviors:\n\n1. **Pair ordering** (B2): mapping order per format.md vs sorted key order\n   per reference.py — exercised divergently by probes 1, 5, 6.\n2. **Backslash escaping** (B5): pass-through per format.md's exhaustive\n   escaping clause vs doubling per reference.py — exercised divergently by\n   probe 4.\n\nNo other probe-exercised behavior is undetermined by format.md or\ndetermined differently (B1, B3, B4, B6, B7 agree, as argued clause-by-\nclause above). Consequently a faithful clean-room implementation of\nformat.md is expected to diverge from reference.py on probes 1, 4, 5, 6\nand agree on probes 0, 2, 3, 7, 8, 9 — an expectation only; the\nauthoritative item-level comparison (E-B) belongs to the isolated child\nand deterministic integration.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":966,"cache_read_input_tokens":44166,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":966},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ebc03fb7-12c7-49dd-8856-5c7987adf04a","uuid":"db7b03f6-7685-4b83-b25b-527c0cb3f385","timestamp":"2026-08-09T17:06:11.735Z","request_id":"req_011CdsY6TX4JDwoZkGzcPaCT"}
